  "overview": "Named for its distinctive blue PCB, the 'Blue Pill' is a bare-bones breakout for ST's STM32F103 — a genuine 32-bit ARM Cortex-M3 chip that runs circles around AVR-based Arduinos for a similar price, with far more GPIO, real hardware timers, and multiple communication peripherals. The tradeoff is a rougher on-ramp: no Arduino-style bootloader by default (many boards need to be flashed once via ST-Link or a USB-to-serial adapter in bootloader mode), inconsistent documentation across clone batches, and a chip that runs at 3.3V logic throughout — though most GPIOs are usefully 5V-tolerant as inputs.",
  "simpleSummary": "A bare-bones but powerful 32-bit board that outperforms Arduino for a similar price.",
  "specifications": {
    "microcontroller": "STM32F103C8T6, Arm Cortex-M3, 72 MHz",
    "operatingVoltage": "3.3V (most GPIOs are 5V-tolerant as inputs)",
    "flashMemory": "64-128 KB (varies by chip batch)",
    "sram": "20 KB",
    "digitalIOPins": "~37 GPIO across ports A, B, C",
    "connector": "Micro-USB (board-dependent) — often needs external ST-Link for first flash"
  },
